+      "evidence": "Every required section is present and substantive: What it does (three bullets: when it runs, what it reads, what it writes); Inputs / Outputs (two inputs, four outputs including the failure output 'when Phoenix memory cannot be reached, a plain message that says so and no project memory built'); Rules & behavior (six rules with edge cases); Acceptance criteria (five Given/When/Then); Out of scope (six items each naming an owner); Agent intent (ICE) with Goal, Limit, Fails if, Context, Checked by.",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "self_explanation",
+      "verdict": "fail",
+      "evidence": "Most items carry their why (e.g. 'Every read goes through the memory tool, because every part of Garura reads memory that way and the tool is what records where each file came from'; the unreachable rule explains 'An empty result here would look like \"no file fits\"'). Two do not: 'When an answer selects nothing in Phoenix memory (for example a tool memory has no standard for), the record says so in plain words, and the project's memory is built from the other answers. Our default; the sources do not say what happens then.' and 'When a run is repeated (Run initialize again, fn-rerun-initialize), the memory is rebuilt from the new answers, and the record says which files were added and which were taken out.' Also the output 'each with where it came from in Phoenix memory' states no reason for recording the origin.",
+      "fix": "Add the why to each of the three items. For the selects-nothing rule, say why it goes on instead of stopping (for example: 'because one answer with no matching standard does not make the other answers wrong, unlike an unreachable Phoenix memory, where nothing can be trusted'). For the rerun rule, say why the added and removed files are recorded (for example: 'so the product owner sees what the new answers changed before a later play relies on it'). For the origin output, add 'so a later run (fn-883) can compare each file with Phoenix memory and find when it changed'."
